 {"key":"letter:c","show":"c","says":"/k/; /s/ […] Before the letters <i>e</i> or <i>i</i>.","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/k/","like":"s<b>c</b>an","rx":"(?<![s])c(?=[aouáóúâôãõlrt])"},{"when":"Before the letters <i>e</i> or <i>i</i>.","ipa":"/s/","like":"<b>s</b>ack","rx":"c(?=[eiéíê])"}]},
 {"key":"letter:ç","show":"ç","says":"The <b>cedilla</b> (◌̧) indicates that <i>ç</i> is pronounced /s/ […].","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/s/","like":"<b>s</b>ack","rx":"ç"}]},
 {"key":"letter:ch","show":"ch","says":"Portuguese uses digraphs, pairs of letters which represent a single sound different from the sum of their components.","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/ʃ/","like":"<b>sh</b>eep","rx":"ch"}]},
 {"key":"letter:g","show":"g","says":"/ɡ/ […]; /ʒ/ […] Before the letters <i>e</i> or <i>i</i>.","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/ɡ/","like":"a<b>g</b>ain","rx":"g(?=[aoáóâôãõlr])|g(?=u[aoá])"},{"when":"Before the letters <i>e</i> or <i>i</i>.","ipa":"/ʒ/","like":"plea<b>s</b>ure","rx":"g(?=[eiéíê])"}]},
 {"key":"letter:h","show":"h","says":"natively silent […] Silent at the start or at the end of a word. Also part of the digraphs <i>ch</i>, <i>lh</i>, <i>nh</i>.","rows":[{"when":"Silent at the start […] of a word.","ipa":"—","rx":"^h"}]},
 {"key":"letter:j","show":"j","says":"","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/ʒ/","like":"plea<b>s</b>ure","rx":"j"}]},
 {"key":"letter:lh","show":"lh","says":"","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/ʎ/","like":"<i>Italian</i> fi<b>gli</b>o","rx":"lh"}]},
 {"key":"letter:nh","show":"nh","says":"","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/ɲ/","like":"<i>Spanish</i> ni<b>ñ</b>o","rx":"nh"}]},
 {"key":"letter:qu","show":"qu","says":"The digraphs ⟨qu⟩ and ⟨gu⟩, before ⟨e⟩ and ⟨i⟩, may represent both plain or labialised sounds (quebra /ˈkɛbɾɐ/, cinquenta /sĩˈkʷẽtɐ/, guerra /ˈɡɛʁɐ/, sagui /saˈɡʷi/), but they are always labialised before ⟨a⟩ and ⟨o⟩ (quase, quociente, guaraná).","rows":[{"when":"before ⟨e⟩ and ⟨i⟩","ipa":"/k/; /kʷ/","like":"s<b>c</b>an","rx":"qu(?=[eiéíê])"},{"when":"before ⟨a⟩ and ⟨o⟩","ipa":"/kʷ/","rx":"qu(?=[aoáâ])"}]},
 {"key":"letter:r","show":"r","says":"The alveolar flap /ɾ/ is always spelled as a single ⟨r⟩. The other rhotic phoneme of Portuguese, which may be pronounced as a trill [r] or as one of the fricatives [x], [ʁ], or [h], according to the idiolect of the speaker, is either written ⟨rr⟩ or ⟨r⟩ […].","rows":[{"when":"Start of syllable","ipa":"/ʁ/","like":"<i>French</i> <b>r</b>ouge","rx":"^r"},{"when":"Between vowels","ipa":"/ɾ/","like":"a<b>t</b>om (GA)","rx":"(?<=[aeiouáéíóúâêô])r(?=[aeiouáéíóúâêôã])"},{"when":"End of syllable","ipa":"/ɾ/","like":"EP: a<b>t</b>om (GA)","rx":"r$"}]},
 {"key":"letter:rr","show":"rr","says":"","rows":[{"when":"Between vowels","ipa":"/ʁ/","like":"<i>French</i> <b>r</b>ouge","rx":"rr"}]},
 {"key":"letter:s","show":"s","says":"A single <i>s</i> is pronounced voiced [z] between vowels. […] The opposition between the four sibilants /s/, /z/, /ʃ/, /ʒ/ is neutralized at the end of syllables […]. Note that there are two main groups of accents in Portuguese, one in which the sibilants are alveolar at the end of syllables (/s/ or /z/), and another in which they are postalveolar (/ʃ/ or /ʒ/).","rows":[{"when":"Start of syllable","ipa":"/s/","like":"<b>s</b>ack","rx":"^s(?=[aeiouáéíóú])"},{"when":"Between vowels","ipa":"/z/","like":"<b>z</b>ebra","rx":"(?<=[aeiouáéíóúâêô])s(?=[aeiouáéíóúâêôã])"},{"when":"Closed syllables and end of syllable","ipa":"/ʃ/","like":"EP: <b>sh</b>eep","rx":"s$|s(?=[ptcqf])"}]},
 {"key":"letter:ss","show":"ss","says":"","rows":[{"when":"Between vowels","ipa":"/s/","like":"<b>s</b>ack","rx":"ss"}]},
 {"key":"letter:x","show":"x","says":"The letter <i>x</i> may represent /ʃ/, /ks/, /z/, /s/, or [gz] (<i>peixe, fixar, exemplo, próximo, hexágono</i>). It is always pronounced /ʃ/ at the beginning of words and /ks/ at the end of words.","rows":[{"when":"","ipa":"/ʃ/","like":"<b>sh</b>eep","words":["o peixe","a caixa","o lixo"],"mark":"x"},{"when":"","ipa":"/ks/","words":["o táxi"],"mark":"x"},{"when":"","ipa":"/z/","like":"<b>z</b>ebra","words":["o exame"],"mark":"x"},{"when":"","ipa":"/s/","like":"<b>s</b>ack","words":["próximo, próxima"],"mark":"x"}]},
 {"key":"letter:z","show":"z","says":"/z/, /s/, /ʃ/ […] The opposition between the four sibilants /s/, /z/, /ʃ/, /ʒ/ is neutralized at the end of syllables […].","rows":[{"when":"Start of syllable […] Between vowels","ipa":"/z/","like":"<b>z</b>ebra","rx":"z(?=[aeiouáéíóú])"},{"when":"Closed syllables and end of syllable","ipa":"/ʃ/","like":"EP: <b>sh</b>eep","rx":"z$"}]}
],
"rules": [
 {"key":"rule:stress-penult","show":"casa · livro · cidade","kind":"stress","name":"the syllable before the last","says":"Most multisyllabic words are stressed on the penult. […] The endings <i>-a(s)</i>, <i>-e(s)</i>, <i>-o(s)</i>, <i>-am</i>, <i>-em</i>, <i>-ens</i> are unstressed. The stressed vowel of words with such endings is assumed to be the first one before the ending itself: <i>bo<b>ni</b>ta</i>, <i>bo<b>ni</b>tas</i>, <i><b>gen</b>te</i> […].","words":["a casa","o livro","a cidade"]},
 {"key":"rule:stress-final","show":"falar · hotel · jardim","kind":"stress","name":"the last syllable","says":"Words with other endings are regarded as oxytone by default: <i>vi<b>ver</b></i>, <i>jar<b>dim</b></i>, <i>vi<b>vi</b></i>, <i>bam<b>bu</b></i> […]. They require an accent when they are stressed on a syllable other than their last: <i><b>tá</b>xi</i>, <i><b>fá</b>cil</i> […].","words":["falar","o hotel","o jardim","o táxi","fácil"]},
 {"key":"rule:stress-written","show":"café · você · número","kind":"stress","name":"an accent where the stress is not the expected one","says":"All words stressed on the antepenult take an accent mark. […] If the word happens to be stressed elsewhere, it requires an accent mark: <i>se<b>rá</b></i>, <i>se<b>rás</b></i>, <i>a<b>té</b></i> […].","words":["o café","você","até","o número","a música"]},
 {"key":"rule:acute-circumflex","show":"á é ó · â ê ô","kind":"accents","name":"the acute and the circumflex","says":"The <b>acute accent</b> (◌́) and the <b>circumflex accent</b> (◌̂) indicate that a vowel is stressed and the quality of the accented vowel and, more precisely, its height: <i>á</i>, <i>é</i>, and <i>ó</i> are low vowels (except in nasal vowels); <i>â</i>, <i>ê</i>, and <i>ô</i> are high vowels. They also distinguish a few homographs: <i>por</i> \"by\" with <i>pôr</i> \"to put\" […].","heads":["Grapheme","Pronunciation"],"pairs":[["á","/a/"],["â","/ɐ/ (/ɐ̃/)"],["é","/ɛ/"],["ê","/e/ (/ẽ/, /ɐj/)"],["í","/i/ (/ĩ/)"],["ó","/ɔ/"],["ô","/o/ (/õ/)"],["ú","/u/ (/ũ/)"]],"words":["a água","o pé","o avô, a avó","você","três"]},
 {"key":"rule:grave","show":"à","kind":"accents","name":"the grave accent","says":"The <b>grave accent</b> (◌̀) marks the contraction of two consecutive vowels in adjacent words (crasis), normally the preposition <i>a</i> and an article or a demonstrative pronoun: <i>a</i> + <i>aquela</i> = <i>àquela</i> \"at that\", <i>a</i> + <i>a</i> = <i>à</i> \"at the\". It can also be used when indicating time: \"às 4 horas\" = \"at 4 o'clock\". It does not indicate stress.","words":["a"]},
 {"key":"rule:amos","show":"falamos · falámos","kind":"accents","name":"we speak, we spoke","says":"In European Portuguese, a distinction is made in the first person plural of verbs in <i>-ar</i>, between the present tense ending <i>-amos</i> /ˈɐmuʃ/ and the preterite <i>-ámos</i> /ˈamuʃ/. As these are pronounced identically in Brazilian Portuguese, this accent is not used.","words":["falar"]}
],
